[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was marked known: one issue, one fix. Its reports say otherwise.
Read for what happened to the date, not for their subject line, they hold three problems with
three different fixes. Each problem has a ticket of its own.

Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October: the parser fix shipped, and the booking-v3 form was
retired, so its hint will not be fixed. Those two tickets are closed with it.

<Callout kind="warn">
Closing #4471 did not reach <code>shift</code>. Neither the parser fix nor retiring booking-v3 touches the
time zone of an invite, so these reports keep coming. Its ticket stays open.
</Callout>

## The problems in #4471

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} />

## How the reports are counted

The way the desk counts, with its own code: `groupByCause` in `causes.ts` and the desk's notes in
`desk-notes.json`. The page adds no rule of its own for `parser` and `hint`.

1. **`parser`**: the tenant writes the day first, and the report did not come through a booking-v3
   form (`causes.ts`).
2. **`hint`**: the tenant writes the day first, and the report came through a booking-v3 form,
   whose hint prints `MM/DD/YYYY` (`causes.ts`).
3. **`shift`**: the desk read the report as a one-day move (`desk-notes.json`). A month and a day
   that trade places never land one day apart, so this is a problem of its own. The desk counts a
   report where it is filed and footnotes the ones it read differently, so these reports count
   under their cause and again here.
4. **`unplaced`**: no tenant on file, or the tenant does not write the day first. No ticket until
   someone reads them.

Intake's tag is a guess from the reporter's browser. The table shows it next to the problem so
the gap stays visible: an en-US browser is not a booking-v3 form.

## What each ticket asks for

**`parser`**: the shared parser reads every date month first. For a tenant who writes the day
first, any date with both numbers at 12 or under comes out with day and month swapped:
booked 2 July, invite for 7 February. Fix: parse with the tenant's locale, the way
`dayFirst()` already asks the platform. Closed, fixed: `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`.

**`hint`**: booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field, and the form then reads the
date the tenant's way, day first. People type what the hint says and get day and month
swapped. Fix: print the hint from the tenant's locale. The parser fix does not reach this one.
Closed, won't fix: the booking-v3 form is retired, so nobody types into that hint any more.

**`shift`**: the date is right but lands one day early for some readers: the same meeting shows
Tuesday in Lisbon and Monday in Toronto, and an invite can arrive "before" the booking. That is
a date sent as midnight in one time zone and shown in another. Fix: send all-day dates as dates,
not as instants. Open: nothing engineering shipped for #4471 touches it.

## For the desk

- Matching on subject merged these ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)). The same
  subject line turns up under different problems, so the subject cannot tell them apart. Read the
  body, or at least the tenant and the way in, before you merge.
- Keep a report's ticket with its problem. When a new report comes in for #4471, file it by cause
  as the desk always has, and add it to that problem's ticket; a one-day move goes on `shift` too.
- Ticket states come from engineering's update (`engineering-4471.json`); a problem it did not
  name stays open. When engineering closes `shift`, add it there and the table follows.
- The numbers on this page are built from the reports at render time. When the desk merges more
  reports into #4471, they move on their own.

